<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.25%" pitch="-2.80%">
            Psalm sixty two speaks in the language of quiet waiting: For God alone my soul waits in silence; from Him comes my salvation.<break time="500ms"/>
      The Psalm repeats that posture more than once, as though the soul must be taught to return there again and again.<break time="500ms"/>
      He alone is my rock and my salvation, my fortress; I shall not be greatly shaken.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The repetition is part of the gift, because the heart often needs truth to come more than once before it begins to settle under it.<break time="500ms"/>
      Waiting in silence is not useless passivity in the Psalms; it is directed expectancy anchored in God's character.<break time="500ms"/>
      Psalm sixty two slows the inner life by giving it one place to lean and one source from which help truly comes.<break time="1800ms"/>
      The turning point comes by God's faithfulness.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The Psalm repeats quiet waiting because the soul learns rest by returning.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Silence becomes safe when it is silence before God.<break time="1000ms"/>
      You can let your soul wait without forcing an outcome tonight.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>